const pageData = JSON.parse("{\"subject\":\"Physics\",\"title\":\"Mass, density, and inertia\",\"eyebrow\":\"\",\"introduction\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"contentHtml\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eMatter, this is one of the most fundamental concept in physics, dark matter, anti-matter, the problems of matter persists to current days in advanced physics. In Newtonian mechanics, the definition of matter and mass is a bit equivocal yet acceptable.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eOur universe is composed of matter, you and I, the Earth and the Moon, anything you can think of and is realistic, is considered as matter. In Newtonian mechanics, matter always has certain mass, since mass is the measure of amount of matter as defined, and mass is represented by m. In contemporary society, we utilize gram, kilogram, and ton, in most scenarios, as the units of mass. However, there are some informal and daily-life unit of mass: pound, 斤(jin, 0.5kg).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eAnd we know that in 1D, 2D, and 3D spaces, matter has their own shapes (indeed, the matter we've been exposed to so far is 3D, despite that you can approximate some of them with 1D or 2D shapes).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e1D: line (length);\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e2D: plane (area);\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003e3D: body (volume).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThus, we can divide mass by shape quantity in space so that we'll know the efficiency of how matter takes up space.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003ctable\\u003e\\n\\u003cthead\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDimension\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003cth\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eDensity\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/th\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003c/thead\\u003e\\n\\u003ctbody\\u003e\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e1D (line, length)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\\\lambda=\\\\frac{m}{L}\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e2D (plane, area)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\\\sigma=\\\\frac{m}{A}\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctr\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e3D (body, volume)\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003ctd\\u003e\\\\rho=\\\\frac{m}{V}\\u003c/td\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tr\\u003e\\n\\u003c/tbody\\u003e\\u003c/table\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eThese are linear (kg/m), surface (kg/m^{2}), and volume (kg/m^{3}) densities, respectively. Ideally, to simplify problems, we oftentimes assume that most objects possess uniform densities so that the equations above are applicable and questions won't involve calculus. However, in reality, most objects do not have uniform density but inuniform density; in these cases, we'll need do intergral to obtain the mass. \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eWe use 3D volume density as an example. From m=/rho V, a new equation is obtained: \\ndm=/rhodV, m=\\\\int\\\\rhodV.\\n\\\\rho(r) is a function of density with respect to r; dV=Adr.\\nm=\\\\int_{0}^{R}\\\\rho(r)Adr.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eSpecific calculations depend on the shape and density information of the situations, but this serves as a general direction to follow when calculating mass. The final concept is inertia, a more blurry concept that cannot be easily be quantified. In Newtonian mechanics, inertia refers to the resistance of the matter toward any external change of the motion state of the matter. For instance, if a car travels fast on highway and needs to stop since there's another parked car on the front road 10 meters away, the driver needs to stamp the brake so that the car takes a while to slow down. Now consider a new scenario in which a truck is driving on highway, and this time, according to our common knowledge, the truck driver needs to brake the car ealier so that it can stop before hitting the parked car. This reflects the property of inertia that inertia is larger for matter with larger mass; in another word, the motion of matter with larger mass is harder to be changed. Despite that there are no explicit formula describing the relationship between inertia and mass, we believe that inertia is proportional to mass. \\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eIndeed, inertia is one of the physics concepts that haven't been well quantified  (serves more like a premise/assumption based on experimental proof).\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\\u003cp\\u003eInertia, in quick summary, is the tendency of matter remaining their existing motion state (constant velocity) that also resists external change. To better understand this, see the upcoming page of acceleration, force, and Newton's three laws.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\n\",\"children\":[],\"parentTitle\":\"Position vector, velocity vector, and speed\",\"parentUrl\":\"/physics/interesting-topics/classical-mechanics/kinematics/position-vector-velocity-vector-and-speed/\"}");
